feels a little odd to sing Halle Halle  hallelujah after weeping and gnashing of  teeth let us pray holy God break open  your word so that we can see the gospel  the good news in the midst of these  tough texts may the words of my mouth  and the meditations of each one of our  hearts be pleasing in your sight Oh God  you are our rock and our Redeemer amen  this is how I wake up in the morning  lovely harp it beckons me from the side  of my bed where I can gently wake up and  face a new day but if I had to give a  sound effect for these texts today this  is what it sounds like if you've come  here today seeking solace and comfort if  you hope this sermon is going to sound  like that beautiful harp music you need  to wait about nine minutes and 30  seconds till I get to that because i  just cannot ignore the intense wake up  call of the gospel this morning it is a  crazy loud alarm that demands our  attention let's start with Paul and his  words to the Thessalonians the day of  the Lord will come like a thief in the  night he implores them to keep awake and  be sober remember all of the  speculations about the end of the world  back in May what hype and yet most of us  paid attention to the hype didn't we  maybe if just to joke a little bit about  those poor college seniors who might not  get to actually graduate should the  world end a few days before commencement  but this was no joking matter for some  not hype but instead deep conviction  followed by devastation when it didn't  happen as predicted Markley would  suggest however  that the question shouldn't be when is  the world going to end the question  instead is how shall we live in the  meantime last Sunday after worship I met  a woman who is visiting in our  congregation she'd returned to Yale with  her husband for his 50th class reunion  from Yale Law School she said it was  great being back at Yale but she was  surprised at what she discovered about  this class of theirs how many classmates  had died since their last reunion most  of them well into their 70s their group  was shrinking showing its age but even  more surprising to this woman was the  group of alums back for their 60th class  reunion such a tiny group she said and  they looked so much older so much more  weary than our class in just 10 short  years and then she looked at me with  pretty wide eyes time is running out I  just realized that our days are numbered  her wake-up call of mortality convinced  her all the more that she was supposed  to risk comfort convenient and in her  case even arrest she explained that if  she hadn't been with us that Sunday  morning she really would have wished to  be with her son and circling in the  circle of protesters in Washington DC  protesting the Keystone XL pipeline she  shared that her son had grown very  passionate in his opposition of this  proposed pipeline and the threat of its  effects on the environment he was going  to washington d.c a second time last  weekend to protest and she wished she  could be there with him she was prepared  to be arrested she said it my place in  life what do I have to lose I have no  reason not to do this it's not as if  this woman had been asleep her whole  life but last weekend she realized in a  new way that her days are indeed  numbered it was time to live out her  convictions now the Apostle Paul pleads  so then let us not fall asleep as others  do but let us keep awake Paul's wake-up  call is not a call to fear he assures  his community that they are already  children of light that God has destined  them for salvation through Christ but  Paul warns with the language of that  blaring alarm keep awake trust in  Christ's coming but don't just sit there  there is work to be done Martin Luther  is said to have promised even if I knew  that tomorrow was the end of the world I  would plant an apple tree today and this  inspiring visitor in our midst last  weekend is living sentiment all or she  is living that sentiment all the more  urgently now a second wake-up call comes  of course in Matthew's Gospel today we  hear this parable about three slaves  each is given a large large sum of money  the first to invest their money get a  huge return on this investment and  please their master very much the third  slave is terrified of this kind of  reckless investing he doesn't want to  risk everything and he buries his money  in the ground obviously it doesn't  multiply there in the dirt and his  master is furious there are many many  interpretations of this parable most  commentary side with one of these  characters but rarely the same one  interestingly enough some believe the  master is the one to favor in this story  he risked large sums of money by giving  it away to the slaves he gave them the  opportunity to risk he was clearly the  wise one others believe that this  parable is about the two slaves who  doubled their money sure such a large  return on their investment involved  immense risk but to please the master  the risk is worth it finally we could  side with the third slave the one  condemned to outer darkness where there  will be weeping and gnashing of teeth  but he was appropriately cautious don't  you think he didn't want to lose even a  fraction of that large sum of money  gifted to him and he had the guts to  stand up to the master at the end told  him just how harsh she was to his slaves  we can sympathize with him to this  parable is about risk no matter who you  side with and our version too faithful  risk God's call to faithful risk and  Christ's example a faithful risk I will  continue to say faithful risk because as  one of your chaplains I cannot encourage  all risk risky behavior particularly  among students is unfortunately what  keeps your chaplains awake at night but  maybe the opposite of faithful risk is  fear driven by perfectionism fear that  paralyzes us at the very moment when God  is throwing open doors and asking us to  walk through them fear that makes us  question our worth as children of God  fear that can sound louder than the yes  of the gospel this kind of spiritual  fear so has your chaplains concerned and  while I may be as risk-averse as many of  you I really believe each of us is  called to faithful risk in this kind of  risk we are fully alive in Christ what  is faithful risk I think it is the  father who never gave up hope of a  reunion with his sons despite decades of  being cut off from them following a  bitter divorce it is his eldest son who  finally worked up the nerve to find him  and call him and ask to meet his father  it is the yes over the phone and finally  getting to meet the grandchildren he  didn't even know he had faithful risk is  Jesus weeping for his dear friend  turning his face toward Jerusalem eating  with the wrong people for giving  everyone faithful risk is saying I'm  sorry or I love you or I need  help not knowing just what the other  person might say in return faithful risk  for some is even walking through these  chapel doors some of us are naturally  quite cautious some of us are naturally  risk takers so you and God need to sort  out what kind of risk will be faithful  for you faithful risk rarely makes  headlines nor is it convenient it is not  risk for our benefit but serves other  people and God it is risky because we  will lose a bit of pride or control in  the process but the gospel wake-up call  is a challenge to ask what we've been  given by God look at where we may have  buried it out of fear and ask ourselves  how we might use these buried treasures  in faithful ways my favorite  interpretation of this gospel parable is  spun in a new way by anna carter  florence she reimagines this story in  which jesus is going on a journey and he  gives three disciples the gospel to one  he gives stories to another he gives  compassion and to the third he gives the  bread of life and cup of salvation the  first disciple ventures out and shares  those stories of Jesus life with others  and they to become disciples the second  gave compassion to others who in turn  would be bearers of compassion as they  are disciples now and the third disciple  digs a hole in the ground and berries  that bread of life and cup of salvation  this sounds a little bit different than  Matthews version in which the first two  slaves sound like smarmy investment  bankers but this isn't about them it's  about that third guy who out of fear  buries his treasure and not his own  treasure God's treasure temporarily  entrusted to him  bread and cup for his neighbors starving  for these gifts of God someone somewhere  along the line may have shared these  same gospel treasures with you and now  that these treasures are in our hands  what will we do with them bury them out  of fear dump them on the ground or risk  losing our pride comfort or control by  speaking up when injustice is happening  or going out of our way to offer  compassion or coming out as Christians  even if we're not sure what others might  think or noticing who is hungry and  sharing bread well these may sound risky  to you John Buchanan puts it in this  framework the greatest risk of all it  turns out is not to risk anything not to  care deeply and profoundly enough about  anything to invest deeply to give your  heart away and in the process risk  everything that is the biggest risk to  not risk anything now it's been nine  minutes and 30 seconds so it's time for  some reassurance because I know that  many of you are ridiculously tired right  now and now you feel like you have to  put faithful risk on your to-do list and  then feel really guilty when you don't  have time or energy and you're just not  able to do it if everything I've said so  far feels impossible just forget it  forget the thief in the night forget the  weeping and gnashing of teeth forget  having to make the right decisions by  yourself forget where you buried that  treasure if you remember anything  remember Paul's words to the  Thessalonians not mine they are  consolation for those of us who love  risk and those of us who check the locks  on the doors of our hearts several times  a day there are good news for today  whether we are awake or asleep we may  live with  Jesus Christ therefore encourage one  another and build up each other as  indeed you are doing amen
 

